{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310678,"nonce":"mz+tXpJb+bS52hDlXdCvufII2ok=","title":"Q4_K_M 4-block paired SDOT extension of the 2-block winning pattern (acb80e5) washed out under standalone microbench. 4-block is 3% SLOWER ...","message":"`/tmp/q4km_paired_bench.rs` mocks the inner kernel for k=2048 (8 blocks per\nrow), 256 synthetic rows, 100 iterations. Three runs (M1 Pro):\n\n| Version | Run 1 | Run 2 | Run 3 | Median | vs 1-block |\n|---------|-------|-------|-------|--------|------------|\n| 1-block | 3.32 ms | 3.20 ms | 3.21 ms | 3.21 | 1.00x |\n| 2-block | 3.06 ms | 3.07 ms | 3.17 ms | **3.07** | **0.957x (+4.3%)** |\n| 3-block | 3.12 ms | 3.18 ms | 5.71 ms | 3.18 | 0.993x (sub-noise + unstable) |\n| 4-block | 3.10 ms | 3.09 ms | 3.11 ms | 3.10 | 0.966x (+3.4%) |\n\nKey observations:\n- 2-block is the fastest tile, confirming the acb80e5 +3.04% Voronoi win.\n- 4-block is **3% slower than 2-block** in 2 of 3 runs (3.10/3.07 = 1.010\n  to 3.11/3.07 = 1.013) — not faster.\n- 3-block's run 3 spike (5.71 ms) is an L1 line-alignment instability;\n  3-element tile across 8-block row leaves a 2-block tail with odd\n  access pattern.\n\n\nNEON has 32 vector registers but per p-iter the 2-block kernel uses ~14\nof them transiently (8 X loads + 8 weight nibbles + 4 accumulators).\n4-block doubles transient pressure to ~28 — at the edge of the register\nfile. The compiler must spill/reload some accumulator partials each\np-iter, eating the ILP win that wider tiles were supposed to deliver.\n\nM1's OoO scheduler also has a finite ILP window (~8-10 issuable in-\nflight SDOTs). 2-block keeps 8 chains alive concurrently; 4-block tries\nto keep 16 alive and stalls on register-rename pressure.\n\n\nThis is the **4th** \"mirror existing winning pattern\" optimization that\nwashed out this session:\n\n1. [[bugs-q6k-partial-unroll-no-win]] — Q6_K 8-way partial-acc unroll\n2. [[bugs-f32-flash-gqa-no-win]] — F32 flashAttnDecode GQA-aware restructure\n3. [[bugs-swiglu-fusion-no-win]] — SwiGLU gate+up matmul fusion\n4. This one — Q4_K_M 4-block paired SDOT\n\nStanding lesson: architectural symmetry does not guarantee a win; the\nwin depends on the actual per-overhead fraction AND the hardware\nsubstrate's ILP / register / cache limits. **Microbench overhead BEFORE\nwriting 100+ LOC mirror.** This bench cost ~15 minutes and saved an\nafternoon of production kernel work + Voronoi A/B that would have\nwashed out.\n\n\nThe `/tmp/q4km_paired_bench.rs` standalone binary is reusable. To run:\n\n```bash\nrustc -O -C target-cpu=apple-m1 -C target-feature=+dotprod \\\n    /tmp/q4km_paired_bench.rs -o /tmp/q4km_bench\n/tmp/q4km_bench\n```\n\nThe bench requires nightly (uses `#![feature(stdarch_neon_dotprod)]`).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_q4km_4block_paired_no_win` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}